course: dictionary
lesson: 2412
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The sentence around it tells you which meaning the writer wants. Cutting is a good example. «Always use a chopping board when cutting, and cut away from your fingers.» Here cutting is a verb: it means using a sharp tool to divide something.
explain: The same word can work as an adjective, which describes a noun. «The cutting remark made her go quiet.» A remark that hurts is cutting. Wind that is very cold can be cutting too. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Words also show up in phrases. «The team uses the cutting edge technology.» Cutting edge means the newest technology. You cannot work out a phrase by looking at each word on its own, so when a phrase seems strange, read the whole sentence.
fact: As a verb, cutting means using a knife, scissors or other sharp tool to divide or open something now.
fact: A cutting is a small piece cut from a plant so that it grows into a new plant.
fact: In the UK, a cutting is a piece cut out of a newspaper.
fact: As an adjective, cutting describes words that are unkind and hurt, or wind that is very cold.
fact: «Cutting edge» means the newest technology, and «cutting corners» means doing something badly to save time.
exercise: In «a cutting remark», what does cutting mean? | A piece cut out of a newspaper | *Unkind and hurtful | Using scissors to divide paper | A small piece cut from a plant
exercise: In «careful cutting of roots and branches», what does cutting mean? | Doing something badly to save time | *Using a sharp tool to trim or divide | Pushing into a line | A small piece of a plant
exercise: Which phrase means the newest technology? | Cutting remark | Cutting in | Cutting corners | *Cutting edge
test: Gran says, «Put this cutting in a jar of water and it will grow roots.» What is a cutting? | A tiny kind of tree | *A small piece cut from a plant that grows into a new plant | A piece cut out of a newspaper | A sharp knife for vegetables
test: «The cutting wind made the children run home.» What does cutting mean here? | Very slow | *Very cold | Very friendly | Very hot
exam: Which sentence uses cutting as a verb, meaning using a sharp tool? | «The cutting edge of science is exciting.» | *«Always cut away from your fingers when cutting paper.» | «The newspaper cutting was pinned to the wall.» | «The cutting remark hurt her feelings.»
